WN.lb(50,{"mt_LxK9OKZQZX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A sound wave has frequency {=f*10} Hz and wavelength {l} m. What is its speed in m/s?","answer":"f*10*l","hint":"Multiply frequency by wavelength.","explain":"v = {=f*10} × {l} = {=f*10*l} m/s.","params":{"f":[10,40],"l":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You wiggle a rope twice as often, with the same tension. What happens to the wavelength?","choices":["It halves","It doubles","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The rope sets the speed, and v = f × λ.","explain":"The speed is fixed by the rope. Double f means λ must halve so that f × λ stays the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"On a diagram, the distance from a crest to the next trough is 0.5 m. What is the wavelength?","choices":["1 m","0.5 m","0.25 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Crest to trough is only part of a full wave.","explain":"Crest to trough is half a wave, so the wavelength is 2 × 0.5 = 1 m."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A wave travels at {=l*f} m/s with a wavelength of {l} m. What is its frequency in Hz?","answer":"f","hint":"Use frequency = speed ÷ wavelength.","explain":"Frequency = {=l*f} ÷ {l} = {f} Hz.","params":{"l":[2,5],"f":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which change makes a wave on a rope carry more energy without changing its wavelength?","choices":["A bigger amplitude","A higher frequency","A longer rope"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which property changes the height but not the length of the waves?","explain":"A bigger amplitude: the rope moves further from the middle, which takes more energy. Wavelength depends on frequency and speed only."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a transverse wave, the particles move…","choices":["at right angles to the direction the wave travels","along the direction the wave travels","along with the wave to the end"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the red bead on the rope.","explain":"At right angles: the rope moves up and down while the wave moves along."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What do the 'compressions' in a sound wave mean?","choices":["Places where the air particles are squashed close together","The highest points of the wave","Places with no air at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the slinky: some coils bunch up.","explain":"Compressions are where the particles crowd together; rarefactions are where they spread apart."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A duck sits on a pond as ripples pass under it. What does the duck do?","choices":["Bobs up and down but stays in about the same place","Is carried along to the edge by the ripples","Stays completely still"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do waves carry the water along, or just the energy?","explain":"The water, and the duck, just bob up and down. The ripples carry energy across the pond, not the water."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A wave has a frequency of {f} Hz and a wavelength of {l} m. What is its speed in m/s?","answer":"f*l","hint":"Use speed = frequency × wavelength.","explain":"Speed = {f} × {l} = {=f*l} m/s.","params":{"f":[2,9],"l":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which of these is a longitudinal wave?","choices":["Sound travelling through air","Light from a lamp","A ripple on water"],"answer":0,"hint":"In which one do the particles move back and forth along the direction of travel?","explain":"Sound: the air is squashed and stretched along the direction the sound travels. Light and ripples are transverse."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Waves on a rope travel at {=v*2} m/s. You wiggle the end {v} times a second. How long is each wave, in metres?","answer":"2","hint":"Use wavelength = speed ÷ frequency.","explain":"Wavelength = {=v*2} ÷ {v} = 2 m.","params":{"v":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Flick one end of a rope and a bump runs along it. The rope stays where it is. Only the wave moves on, carrying energy.","Amplitude is how far a bit of rope moves from the middle. Wavelength is the length of one whole wave, crest to crest. Frequency is how many waves pass each second, in hertz (Hz).","Speed = frequency × wavelength. If 3 waves pass each second and each is 2 m long, the wave moves 6 m each second.","On a rope the bits move up and down, across the way the wave goes. That is a transverse wave. In sound, the air moves back and forth along the way the wave goes. That is a longitudinal wave."],"adult":["Waves transfer energy, not matter: each particle oscillates about a fixed position. In transverse waves (light, water ripples, waves on a string) the oscillation is perpendicular to the direction of travel; in longitudinal waves (sound, a pushed slinky) it is parallel, giving compressions and rarefactions.","The speed is set by the medium, not by the source. Wiggle faster and the frequency rises, so each wave must be shorter: v = f × λ, with v in m/s, f in Hz and λ in metres. Amplitude is independent of all three.","Common slips: measuring the wavelength from a crest to the next trough (that is half a wavelength), and calling the peak-to-trough height the amplitude (that is twice it)."]},"familyTip":"Tie a rope or a skipping rope to a door handle. Take turns wiggling slowly, then fast. Count the waves you can see each time. Faster wiggles give more, shorter waves.","keyIdea":"A wave carries energy, not material. Speed = frequency × wavelength (v = f × λ). Transverse waves wiggle across the direction of travel; longitudinal waves wiggle along it.","visual":{"type":"wavelab","caption":"Change the amplitude, frequency and speed until your wave matches the faint target wave.","opts":{"a":1.5,"f":1,"v":4,"target":[1,2]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwave","caption":"Flagship lab: the wave rope. Wiggle it yourself, measure a wavelength, and turn it into a slinky.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_q8uN41TdjS":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Two wave graphs look exactly alike. One has \"distance (m)\" on its horizontal axis and the other has \"time (s)\". What does the crest-to-crest gap show on each?","choices":["Wavelength on the distance graph, period on the time graph","Wavelength on both","Period on the distance graph, wavelength on the time graph"],"answer":0,"hint":"A gap along an axis is measured in that axis's unit. Which quantity is a length, and which is a time?","explain":"A gap in metres along the distance axis is a wavelength; a gap in seconds along the time axis is a period. Same shape, different meaning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A buoy bobs up and down {a} times in 10 seconds. What is the frequency, in Hz?","answer":"a/10","hint":"Frequency is the number of oscillations in one second. Divide the count by the time.","explain":"{a} oscillations ÷ 10 s = {=a/10} Hz.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"On a displacement–distance graph, a crest is {a} m from the next trough. What is the wavelength, in metres?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Crest to the next trough covers half of one whole wave.","explain":"Crest to trough is half a wavelength, so the wavelength is 2 × {a} = {=2*a} m.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A displacement–time graph shows crests 0.25 s apart. What is the frequency?","choices":["4 Hz","0.25 Hz","25 Hz"],"answer":0,"hint":"The gap is the period. How many periods of 0.25 s fit in 1 s?","explain":"The period is 0.25 s, so f = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4 Hz: four waves pass each second. 0.25 is the period, not the frequency."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A displacement–distance graph shows {a} whole waves across 12 m. What is the wavelength, in metres?","answer":"12/a","hint":"Divide the total distance by the number of whole waves it holds.","explain":"12 m ÷ {a} waves = {=12/a} m per wave, so λ = {=12/a} m.","params":{"a":[2,6]},"where":"12%a==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"On a displacement–time graph the curve goes from +5 cm at a crest down to −5 cm at a trough. Which statement is right?","choices":["The amplitude is 5 cm","The amplitude is 10 cm","The amplitude is 0 cm, because +5 and −5 cancel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Measure from the zero line, where the point rests, to the top of a crest.","explain":"The amplitude is the largest displacement from rest, 5 cm. Crest to trough, 10 cm, is twice the amplitude."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What does the vertical axis show on both a displacement–distance and a displacement–time graph of a wave?","choices":["How far a point is from its rest position","How far the wave has travelled","The time since the wave started"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both graphs are named \"displacement – something\". Which word belongs to the vertical axis?","explain":"Displacement goes up the side on both graphs: how far a point is above or below where it rests. Only the horizontal axis changes, distance or time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A wave has wavelength 2 m (from its distance graph) and period 0.5 s (from its time graph). What is its speed?","choices":["4 m/s","1 m/s","2.5 m/s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Speed = wavelength ÷ period, because the wave moves one wavelength in each period.","explain":"v = λ ÷ T = 2 m ÷ 0.5 s = 4 m/s. Multiplying 2 × 0.5 = 1 would treat the period as if it were the frequency."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"On a displacement–time graph, the crests are 2 s apart. Someone writes \"wavelength = 2 m\". What went wrong?","choices":["A time graph gives the period, 2 s, not the wavelength","Nothing: the gap is the wavelength","The wavelength should be half, 1 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the unit on the horizontal axis before naming the gap.","explain":"The horizontal axis is time, so the gap is 2 s, the period. You cannot get a wavelength from a time graph alone; you need a distance graph or the speed."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A wave graph has \"time (s)\" on its horizontal axis. The gap from one crest to the next is…","choices":["the period","the wavelength","the amplitude"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the unit of the horizontal axis. A gap along it is measured in that unit.","explain":"The gap is a time, in seconds, so it is the period: the time for one full oscillation. A wavelength would be a distance, read off a distance axis."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"On a displacement–distance graph, one crest is at {a} m and the next crest is at {=a+b} m. What is the wavelength, in metres?","answer":"b","hint":"One wavelength is the gap between two neighbouring crests. Subtract the two positions.","explain":"{=a+b} − {a} = {b}, so the wavelength is {b} m.","params":{"a":[1,5],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A wave's crests reach {a} cm above the rest line and its troughs reach {a} cm below. What is the amplitude, in cm?","answer":"a","hint":"Amplitude is measured from the rest line, not from the bottom of a trough.","explain":"The amplitude is the biggest displacement from the rest line: {a} cm. Crest to trough is {=2*a} cm, which is twice the amplitude.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"On a displacement–distance graph, a crest is at 1.0 m and the next trough is at 1.5 m. What is the wavelength?","choices":["1.0 m","0.5 m","1.5 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Crest to the next trough is only part of a wave. How much of one?","explain":"Crest to the next trough is half a wave, 0.5 m, so a whole wavelength is 2 × 0.5 = 1.0 m."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"On a displacement–time graph, the crests are at {a} s, {=a+b} s and {=a+2*b} s. What is the period, in seconds?","answer":"b","hint":"The period is the time from one crest to the next on a time graph.","explain":"Each crest comes {b} s after the one before, so the period is {b} s.","params":{"a":[0,3],"b":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Wave graphs often look the same, so always read the label on the bottom axis first. It tells you what the graph shows.","If the bottom axis says distance, the graph is like a photo of the whole wave at one moment. Crest to crest along it gives the wavelength, in metres.","If the bottom axis says time, the graph follows one spot, like a buoy, as it bobs up and down. Crest to crest gives the period: the time for one bob, in seconds.","The side axis shows how far a spot is from where it rests. Measure from the middle line up to a crest. That is the amplitude. Top to bottom is twice as big."],"adult":["Both graphs plot displacement, the distance of a point from its rest position, on the vertical axis. The horizontal axis decides the meaning. Against distance, the curve is the shape of the medium at one instant, and one repeat of the pattern is the wavelength λ. Against time, the curve is the history of a single point, and one repeat is the period T.","Amplitude is the maximum displacement, read from the zero line to a crest (or to a trough). Frequency is the number of oscillations per second, f = 1/T, so a period of 0.5 s means 2 Hz. Combining the two graphs gives the speed: v = λ/T = fλ.","To read a repeat accurately, measure across several waves and divide: if 3 whole waves span 4.5 m, λ = 1.5 m. Any two matching points work, such as two crests, two troughs, or two zeros where the curve rises.","Common mistakes: taking crest to trough as the amplitude (it is twice the amplitude), taking crest to the next trough as a whole wavelength (it is half), calling the gap on a time graph a wavelength, and writing the period where the frequency is asked for. Checking the axis label and the unit catches most of these."]},"familyTip":"Draw a wavy line on paper and label the bottom axis \"distance (m)\". Ask: what is crest to crest? Then relabel it \"time (s)\" and ask again. The drawing is the same, but the answer changes from a wavelength to a period.","keyIdea":"A displacement–distance graph is a snapshot of the whole wave at one instant, so the gap from crest to crest along it is the wavelength. A displacement–time graph follows one point as time passes, so the crest-to-crest gap is the period. On both, the amplitude is measured from the rest line up to a crest.","worked":{"title":"Reading a displacement–time graph","steps":["The bottom axis says time (s), so this graph follows one point and gives the period, not the wavelength.","Crests are at 0.2 s, 1.0 s and 1.8 s. Two crests span 1.8 − 0.2 = 1.6 s for 2 waves, so T = 0.8 s.","The crests reach 3 cm and the troughs −3 cm, so the amplitude is 3 cm, not 6 cm.","Frequency f = 1 ÷ 0.8 s = 1.25 Hz."]},"visual":{"type":"wavelab","caption":"Change the amplitude and frequency until your wave matches the faint target, then read both values off the graph.","opts":{"a":1,"f":2,"v":4,"target":[1.5,1]}},"lab":{"type":"flagwread","caption":"Flagship lab: one wave, two graphs. Measure crest to crest across distance, then across time, and see which gap is the wavelength and which is the period.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oa6bzrjUCU":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You switch off every light in a room with no windows. Can you see your shadow?","choices":["No, there is no light","Yes, shadows are always there","Yes, it gets darker"],"answer":0,"hint":"A shadow is made when something blocks light. Is there any light?","explain":"With no light at all, nothing is blocked and you cannot see, so there is no shadow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A red ball blocks the light. What colour is its shadow?","choices":["Dark, with no colour","Red like the ball","Yellow like the light"],"answer":0,"hint":"A shadow is where the light cannot reach.","explain":"A shadow is just a place with less light, so it is dark. It has the ball's shape but not its colour."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{n} friends each hold up one hand in the torch light. How many hand shadows are on the wall?","answer":"n","hint":"Each hand blocks some light and makes its own shadow.","explain":"Each of the {n} hands blocks light, so there are {n} hand shadows on the wall.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The Moon looks bright at night. Does it make its own light?","choices":["No, sunlight bounces off it","Yes, like a lamp","Yes, but only at night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the shiny spoon. Bright is not the same as making light.","explain":"The Moon makes no light. We see it because light from the sun bounces off it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You move your hand close to the torch. What happens to the shadow?","choices":["It gets bigger","It gets smaller","It goes away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Close to the torch, your hand blocks more of the light.","explain":"Near the torch your hand blocks more light, so the shadow gets bigger."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{a} toys are in the torch light and {b} toys are in a dark box. How many toys make a shadow?","answer":"a","hint":"Only toys in the light can block light.","explain":"Only the {a} toys in the light block it and make shadows. The {b} toys in the dark box make none.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"The sun is behind you. Where is your shadow?","choices":["In front of you","Behind you, near the sun","Nowhere"],"answer":0,"hint":"The shadow is on the side away from the light.","explain":"You block the sunlight coming from behind, so your shadow falls in front of you."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A shadow on the wall is round. Which toy made it?","choices":["A ball","A star","A cup with a handle"],"answer":0,"hint":"A shadow has the same outline as the thing blocking the light.","explain":"A round shadow comes from a round thing, so the ball made it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A torch shines on a teddy and makes a shadow. Where is the teddy's shadow?","choices":["On the wall behind the teddy","Next to the torch","On top of the torch"],"answer":0,"hint":"The teddy blocks the light. Where can the light not reach?","explain":"The light cannot get past the teddy, so the shadow is on the wall behind it, away from the torch."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children stand in the sunshine. Each one makes a shadow. How many shadows are there?","answer":"n","hint":"Each child blocks the sunlight and makes one shadow.","explain":"Every child blocks some light, so {n} children make {n} shadows.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one is a light source?","choices":["A candle flame","A shiny spoon","A teddy"],"answer":0,"hint":"A light source makes its own light.","explain":"A candle flame makes its own light. A spoon only bounces light, and a teddy makes none."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {a} lamps on. You switch off {b} of them. How many lamps still give light?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Only the lamps that are on give light. Take away the ones you switched off.","explain":"{a} lamps take away {b} switched off leaves {=a-b} lamps giving light.","params":{"a":[3,5],"b":[1,2]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Switch off the lamp. It is dark! We need light to see. The sun, a lamp, a torch and a candle all give light.","A shiny spoon is bright, but it does not make light. It only bounces light. The Moon is the same. The sun lights it up.","Shine a torch at the wall. Put your hand in the light. Your hand stops the light, so a dark shape is on the wall. That is a shadow.","Move your hand near the torch. The shadow gets big! Move it near the wall. The shadow gets small. Switch the torch off. No light, no shadow."],"adult":["This is early science about light. The US Next Generation Science Standards ask young children to show that objects in darkness can be seen only when lit (1-PS4-2) and to test what happens when objects are put in the path of a beam of light (1-PS4-3). The English national curriculum returns to it in Year 3: we need light to see, dark is the absence of light, and shadows form when an opaque object blocks light from a light source.","The key words are light source (something that makes its own light), shadow, block, and later opaque and transparent. A shadow falls on the side of the object away from the light. Its outline matches the object, but it has no colour or detail, because it is simply where the light cannot reach. Moving the object closer to the light makes the shadow bigger.","Common mistakes from classroom research: thinking shiny things or the Moon are light sources, when they only reflect light; believing we can still see a little in total darkness; thinking a shadow is a reflection, or a thing that belongs to the object and can be seen in the dark; and expecting the shadow on the same side as the light. Be careful with phrases like 'the dark comes in'. Dark is just no light.","In the lab your child finds the three light sources in a dark room, drags a teddy into a torch beam to make a shadow, switches the torch off to see the shadow go, moves the teddy to grow and shrink the shadow, and matches shadows to the toys that made them."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, make shadow puppets with a torch on the wall. Ask \"Where will the shadow go?\" before you move your hand, then switch the torch off and ask where the shadow went.","keyIdea":"We need light to see. Light comes from a light source, like the sun, a lamp, a torch or a candle. Dark is when there is no light. A shadow is a dark shape that forms when something blocks the light. The shadow is on the side away from the light, and it has the same shape as the thing blocking the light. No light means no shadow.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it make its own light? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["☀ Gives light","✖ No light of its own"],"items":[["The sun",0],["A lamp",0],["A candle flame",0],["A shiny spoon",1],["The Moon",1],["A teddy",1],["A torch that is on",0],["A mirror",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshadow","caption":"Flagship lab: find the light sources in the dark, make a teddy's shadow with a torch, switch it off, make it big and small, and match shadows to toys.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KzbcjtZHb5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which sound is quiet?","choices":["🤫 A whisper","🚒 A fire engine","📣 A shout"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could you only hear if you were very close?","explain":"A whisper is quiet. A fire engine and a shout are loud."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You want a LOUD shaker. What do you do?","choices":["Shake it hard","Shake it gently","Keep it still"],"answer":0,"hint":"A big, strong shake or a tiny shake: which is louder?","explain":"Shake it hard for a loud sound. A gentle shake is quiet, and a still shaker makes no sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You blow softly into a toy trumpet. What is the sound like?","choices":["Quiet","Loud","Very high, like a bird"],"answer":0,"hint":"Soft breath means a gentle push of air. Is that loud?","explain":"A soft breath makes a quiet sound. Blow hard to make it loud."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A tiny bell goes ding. Is its sound high or low?","choices":["High","Low","It has no sound"],"answer":0,"hint":"Little things often make sounds like a bird. Is that high?","explain":"Tiny bells make high sounds. Big bells make low sounds."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which makes a low sound?","choices":["A big bell","A tiny bell","A bird singing"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a deep, booming dong. Which is big?","explain":"A big bell makes a low dong. A tiny bell and a bird make high sounds."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You clap loudly {n} times and quietly {k} times. How many loud claps?","answer":"n","hint":"Count only the claps that were loud, not the quiet ones.","explain":"The loud claps are the hard ones. You clapped loudly {n} times, so there are {=n} loud claps.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"You tap the drum gently. Then you hit it hard. Which is louder?","choices":["The hard hit","The gentle tap","They sound the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is the same drum. What did you change?","explain":"The hard hit is louder. Hitting harder makes a louder sound on the same drum."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A squeaky toy makes a quiet, high squeak. Is it loud?","choices":["No, it is quiet but high","Yes, high means loud","Yes, toys are loud"],"answer":0,"hint":"High and loud are two different things. Listen to how strong it is.","explain":"The squeak is high, but it is quiet. High and low are about the note; loud and quiet are about how strong the sound is."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You blow the trumpet softly {n} times and hard {k} times. How many loud toots?","answer":"k","hint":"Blowing hard makes a loud toot. Count only the hard blows.","explain":"Soft blows are quiet and hard blows are loud. You blew hard {k} times, so there are {=k} loud toots.","params":{"n":[1,4],"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How do you make a loud sound on a drum?","choices":["Hit it hard","Tap it gently","Hold the stick still"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how hard the stick hits the drum skin.","explain":"A hard hit makes a loud sound. A gentle tap makes a quiet sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You bang the drum hard {n} times. Then you tap it gently {k} times. How many quiet taps?","answer":"k","hint":"The gentle taps are the quiet ones. Count only those.","explain":"Hard bangs are loud. Gentle taps are quiet. You tapped gently {k} times, so there are {=k} quiet taps.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You shake the shaker hard {n} times. How many loud shakes is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Hard shakes make loud sounds. Count each hard shake.","explain":"Every hard shake makes a loud sound. You shook it hard {n} times, so that is {=n} loud shakes.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Baby is asleep. How should you sing?","choices":["Very quietly","Very loudly","Shout the song"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which kind of sound would let baby keep sleeping?","explain":"Quiet sounds let baby sleep. Sing very quietly, like a whisper."}],"explain":{"kid":["Bang a drum hard. BOOM! That is loud. Tap it gently. Tap, tap. That is quiet.","A shaker works the same way. Shake it hard for a loud sound. Shake it a little for a quiet sound.","Blow a toy trumpet hard and it is loud. Blow soft and it is quiet. Your voice can shout or whisper too.","Some sounds are high, like a tiny bell or a bird. Some are low, like a big bell or a big drum. High is not the same as loud."],"adult":["In England this sits in the Early Years Foundation Stage. Development Matters lists two goals for 3 and 4 year olds. In Understanding the World, they explore materials and sounds and how they change. In Expressive Arts and Design, they play instruments with more and more control. The science of sound comes later, in Year 4, when children link how loud a sound is to how strong the vibrations are.","The big idea at this age is cause and effect. More force makes a louder sound: a harder hit, a stronger shake or a bigger breath. Less force makes a quieter sound. Pitch is a separate idea. Bigger or longer things usually sound lower, and smaller or shorter things sound higher. One honest catch: blowing harder into a recorder or toy trumpet can make the note higher as well as louder.","Common mistakes: mixing up high with loud and low with quiet, so a soft squeak gets called loud; thinking big instruments are always loud, even when tapped gently; and thinking fast tapping is the same as hard tapping. Use the words loud, quiet, high and low carefully, and keep them apart, so they become different ideas.","In the lab your child sorts sounds into loud and quiet, lifts a drumstick high or low to bang or tap, shakes a shaker gently for a sleeping baby and hard for a band, blows a trumpet softly or hard, and hears a big bell and a tiny bell to tell high from low."]},"familyTip":"Make a pots-and-pans band. Ask your child to play the pan loudly, then very quietly, and ask what they changed. Try a whisper and a shout, then a high squeak and a low growl. Hide an instrument behind your back, play it, and let them guess which one it was.","keyIdea":"You make a sound when you hit, shake or blow something. Hit, shake or blow hard and the sound is loud. Do it gently and the sound is quiet. High and low are different from loud and quiet: a tiny bell rings high, a big bell rings low.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each sound loud or quiet? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Loud","Quiet"],"items":[["🚒 Fire engine",0],["🤫 Whisper",1],["🥁 Drum hit hard",0],["🐱 Cat purring",1],["📣 Shout",0],["🍃 Leaves rustling",1],["🐕 Big dog barking",0],["🥁 Drum tapped gently",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagloud","caption":"Flagship lab: sort loud and quiet sounds, bang or tap a drum, shake a shaker, blow a trumpet, and hear a big bell and a tiny bell.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KTSoXcO7OL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Maya turns the peg on her guitar {n} times to make the string tighter. What happens to the note?","choices":["It gets higher","It gets lower","It gets quieter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a tighter string vibrate faster or slower?","explain":"A tighter string vibrates faster, so the note gets higher. That is how a guitar is tuned.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Drum A is {a} cm across. Drum B is {=a+d} cm across. Their skins are just as tight. Which sounds higher?","choices":["Drum A, the smaller one","Drum B, the bigger one","They sound the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small things vibrate faster than big things.","explain":"Drum A is smaller, so its skin vibrates faster and makes a higher note. Big drums sound lower.","params":{"a":[15,30],"d":[10,25]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which elastic band makes the lowest note?","choices":["A thick, loose band","A thin, tight band","A thin, loose band"],"answer":0,"hint":"Thick and loose both make the vibrations slower.","explain":"A thick, loose band vibrates slowest, so it has the lowest pitch. Thin and tight bands sound higher."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says a high sound is always a loud sound. Is he right?","choices":["No, pitch and loudness are different","Yes, high means loud","Yes, low means quiet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you sing a high note very quietly?","explain":"No. Pitch depends on how fast something vibrates. Loudness depends on how big the vibrations are."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Faster vibrations make a sound…","choices":["Higher in pitch","Lower in pitch","Quieter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the short ruler twang.","explain":"Faster vibrations make a higher pitch. Slower vibrations make a lower pitch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"On a xylophone, which bars make the high notes?","choices":["The short bars","The long bars","The heavy bars"],"answer":0,"hint":"Short things vibrate faster.","explain":"The short bars vibrate faster, so they make the high notes. The long bars make the low notes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two bottles have the same amount of water. You blow gently across one and hard across the other. What is different?","choices":["How loud it is","How high it is","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"The air column is the same length in both. What does blowing harder change?","explain":"Same water means the same column of air, so the same pitch. Blowing harder makes it louder."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Your twanged ruler makes a low note. How can you make it higher?","choices":["Make it stick out less","Make it stick out more","Twang it more gently"],"answer":0,"hint":"A shorter end vibrates faster.","explain":"Make it stick out less. A shorter end vibrates faster and gives a higher note. Twanging gently only makes it quieter."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ruler A sticks out {a} cm over the table. Ruler B sticks out {=a+d} cm. You twang both. Which note is higher?","choices":["Ruler A, the shorter one","Ruler B, the longer one","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one will vibrate faster: the short end or the long end?","explain":"Ruler A sticks out less, so it vibrates faster. Faster vibrations make a higher pitch.","params":{"a":[8,15],"d":[3,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Bottle A has {a} cm of water. Bottle B has {=a+d} cm of water. You blow across the tops. Which note is higher?","choices":["Bottle B, with more water","Bottle A, with less water","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"When you blow, the air vibrates. Which bottle has the shorter column of air?","explain":"Bottle B has more water, so less air above it. A shorter column of air vibrates faster and gives a higher note.","params":{"a":[3,8],"d":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What makes every sound?","choices":["Something vibrating","Something getting hot","Air staying still"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put your hand on your throat and hum. What do you feel?","explain":"All sounds are made by vibrations. When you hum, your vocal cords vibrate and you can feel the buzz."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ali hits a drum harder. What changes?","choices":["It gets louder","It gets higher","It gets lower"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hitting harder makes the vibrations bigger. Does that change the speed or the size?","explain":"A harder hit makes bigger vibrations, so the sound is louder. The pitch stays the same."}],"explain":{"kid":["Hold a ruler on a table and twang the end. It wobbles up and down very fast. That is a vibration. All sounds are made by things that vibrate.","Pitch is how high or low a sound is. A whistle is high. A big drum is low. When something vibrates faster, its pitch is higher.","Make the ruler stick out less and twang it again. It vibrates faster, and the note goes up. A thin, tight elastic band sounds higher than a thick, loose one.","Loud is not the same as high. Hit a drum harder and it gets louder, but the note stays the same. Only faster or slower vibrations change the pitch."],"adult":["In England, Year 4 science asks children to say how sounds are made, linking them to something that vibrates. They look for patterns between the pitch of a sound and the object that made it. They also look for patterns between volume and the strength of the vibrations.","The key words are vibration, pitch, high, low, volume and loud. Pitch depends on the frequency: how many vibrations there are each second. Short rulers, thin or tight bands, small drums and short columns of air all vibrate faster. Volume depends on how big the vibrations are, not how fast.","Common mistakes to watch for: treating high as loud and low as quiet; thinking a thick or big object makes a higher note; and thinking a sound can happen with nothing vibrating. Bottles also cause muddles. Blow across one with more water and the note is higher, as the air column is shorter. Tap it and the note is lower.","In the lab your child twangs a ruler at four lengths and tunes an elastic band to its highest note. They put the notes of four bottles in order and hit a drum softly and hard, to see that loudness changes but pitch does not. Then they sort objects into high or low pitch."]},"familyTip":"Stretch elastic bands of different thickness over an open box and pluck them. Which is highest? Then twang a ruler on the table edge, making it stick out less each time. Ask: did the note go up or down? Did it vibrate faster or slower? Finish by humming high and low with a hand on your throat.","keyIdea":"Every sound is made by something vibrating. Pitch is how high or low a sound is, and it depends on how fast the object vibrates: faster vibrations make a higher pitch. Short, thin, tight or small objects vibrate faster, so they sound higher; long, thick, loose or big ones sound lower. Loudness is different: bigger vibrations make a louder sound at the same pitch.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"High pitch or low pitch? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["High pitch","Low pitch"],"items":[["a bird tweeting",0],["a lion roaring",1],["a short ruler twang",0],["a long ruler twang",1],["a thin, tight string",0],["a big bass drum",1],["a small bell",0],["a thick, loose band",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpitch","caption":"Flagship lab: twang a ruler, tune an elastic band, order bottle notes, hit a drum soft and hard, and sort high and low.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_p6frRFuxS6":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Translucent means…","choices":["some light passes, but the view is blurry","perfect clear detail","no light at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of frosted glass or tissue over a torch.","explain":"Translucent lets light through as a blurry glow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Clear glass is…","choices":["transparent","opaque","only translucent"],"answer":0,"hint":"You can read a sign through a clear window.","explain":"Clear glass is transparent."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Thick card is…","choices":["opaque","transparent","always translucent"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hold a thick card in front of a torch and see if light gets past.","explain":"Thick card stops the torch beam, so it counts as opaque."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Frosted tissue is…","choices":["translucent","opaque wood","perfectly transparent"],"answer":0,"hint":"You get a glow, not a sharp view.","explain":"Frosted tissue is translucent."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You correctly sort {n} materials into transparent, translucent or opaque. How many did you sort?","answer":"n","hint":"The number you sorted is the answer.","explain":"You sorted {n} materials.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A kit has {n} samples. {k} are transparent. How many are not transparent?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract transparent from the total.","explain":"{n} minus {k} transparent leaves {=n-k}.","params":{"n":[6,10],"k":[1,3]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Wood is usually…","choices":["opaque","transparent","translucent like tissue"],"answer":0,"hint":"You cannot see through a wooden door.","explain":"Wood blocks light, so it is opaque."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why use a torch to test?","choices":["to see if light passes, blurs or stops","to make the material louder","because colour never matters for light"],"answer":0,"hint":"The beam shows how the material treats light.","explain":"A torch shows clear view, blurry glow, or blocked light."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Transparent means…","choices":["you can see clearly through it","light is fully blocked","it is always metal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Imagine looking through a clear window at a tree outside.","explain":"Transparent materials give a clear view through them."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Opaque means…","choices":["light is blocked","you see every detail","must be clear plastic"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a thick book in front of a torch.","explain":"Opaque materials stop the light beam."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You test {n} materials with a torch. How many materials did you test?","answer":"n","hint":"The count of tested materials is the answer.","explain":"You tested {n} materials.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A tray has {n} materials. {k} are opaque. How many are not opaque?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the opaque count from the total.","explain":"{n} minus {k} opaque leaves {=n-k} that are not opaque.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[2,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order from most light through to least.","items":["transparent clear glass","translucent frosted tissue","opaque thick card"],"hint":"Clear view first, then blurry, then blocked.","explain":"Transparent → translucent → opaque."}],"explain":{"kid":["Transparent means you can see clearly through it.","Translucent means some light gets through, but it looks blurry.","Opaque means the light is blocked. You cannot see through it.","Test with a torch: clear view, blurry glow, or no light past."],"adult":["KS1 science: light and materials. Words: transparent, translucent, opaque, beam, torch, clear, blurry, block.","Common mistakes: calling every clear-looking thing transparent; mixing translucent with opaque; thinking opaque means you can still read through it; skipping a torch test.","In the lab your child shines through glass, tissue and card, sorts all three, predicts wood is opaque, then tries challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Shine a torch through clear plastic, tissue paper and a book. Ask: 'Can you see the torch shape clearly, only a glow, or nothing?' Sort three more household materials the same way.","keyIdea":"Materials treat light in three ways. Transparent materials let you see clearly through them. Translucent materials let some light through but the view is blurry. Opaque materials block light so you cannot see through them.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Light through materials — fair idea or myth? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["clear glass is transparent",0],["every material is transparent",1],["frosted tissue is translucent",0],["opaque means you can read through it",1],["thick card blocks the beam",0],["wood is usually opaque",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtto","caption":"Flagship lab: shine through clear, frosted and card; sort three classes; predict wood; then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9roOYvUvad":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Hz × m gives…","choices":["m/s","Hz only","no unit"],"answer":0,"hint":"What unit does the product of frequency and wavelength carry?","explain":"Frequency in Hz (1/s) times length in m gives speed in m/s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"To find frequency from v and λ use…","choices":["f = v ÷ λ","f = v × λ","f = v + λ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rearrange v = f × λ for f.","explain":"Divide both sides by λ: f = v ÷ λ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"f = {a} Hz, λ = {b} m. v?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply frequency by wavelength to get speed in metres per second.","explain":"v = {a} × {b} = {=a*b} m/s.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"2 kHz equals…","choices":["2000 Hz","2 Hz","0.002 Hz"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember kilo means one thousand when converting kilohertz to hertz.","explain":"1 kHz = 1000 Hz, so 2 kHz = 2000 Hz."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Correct first step on mixed units is…","choices":["convert to Hz and m","add f and λ","skip units and guess"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fix units before the product.","explain":"Convert first so v = f × λ uses Hz and metres."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"v = {a} m/s and f = {a} Hz. Wavelength λ in m?","answer":"1","hint":"Divide speed by frequency when both values match; λ becomes 1 m.","explain":"When v and f are the same number, λ = v ÷ f = {a} ÷ {a} = 1 m.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"This lesson’s key skill vs wave-properties lab is…","choices":["calculating and rearranging v = f × λ with units","only wiggling a rope with no numbers","drawing transverse vs longitudinal only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Focus on the calculation and unit work.","explain":"Here you calculate and rearrange with coherent units. Rope and wave-type demos live in the properties lesson."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"λ = {a} m and f = {b} Hz. How much is v?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply wavelength by frequency; the product is speed in m/s.","explain":"v = {a} × {b} = {=a*b} m/s.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Using λ = v × f instead of λ = v ÷ f…","choices":["gives a huge wrong wavelength","always still correct","converts units for free"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check which operation the rearrange needs.","explain":"Wavelength is speed divided by frequency. Multiplying inflates the answer."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Wave speed v equals…","choices":["f × λ","f + λ","f − λ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Speed is a product of frequency and wavelength.","explain":"The wave equation is v = f × λ. Adding or subtracting mixes the quantities wrongly."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"f = {a} Hz and λ = {b} m. Speed v in m/s?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Multiply the frequency in hertz by the wavelength in metres.","explain":"v = {a} × {b} = {=a*b} m/s.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["v = f + λ","v = f × λ","λ = v ÷ f"],"answer":0,"hint":"Spot the false link between v, f and λ.","explain":"Speed is not the sum of frequency and wavelength. The true links are v = f × λ and λ = v ÷ f."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"v = {a} m/s and f = {b} Hz. Wavelength λ in m?","answer":"a/b","hint":"Rearrange: wavelength equals speed divided by frequency (λ = v ÷ f).","explain":"λ = {a} ÷ {b} = {=a/b} m.","params":{"a":[200,400],"b":[50,100]},"where":"a%b==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Before calculating with 2 kHz and 15 cm you should…","choices":["convert to 2000 Hz and 0.15 m","keep kHz and cm mixed","add 2 and 15 for speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put frequency in Hz and wavelength in metres.","explain":"2 kHz = 2000 Hz and 15 cm = 0.15 m. Then v = 2000 × 0.15 = 300 m/s."}],"explain":{"kid":["A wave has a speed v, a frequency f (how many waves per second) and a wavelength λ (the length of one wave). They link by v = f × λ.","If you know two of them, you can find the third. Speed is frequency times wavelength. Wavelength is speed divided by frequency. Frequency is speed divided by wavelength.","Units matter. Frequency in hertz (Hz) and wavelength in metres (m) give speed in metres per second (m/s). Change kilohertz to hertz and centimetres to metres before you multiply.","Do not add frequency and wavelength. Speed is a product, not a sum."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. KS3 waves / NGSS HS-PS4-1: calculate and rearrange v = f × λ with coherent units. This is the calculation lesson, not the rope-properties lab.","Words: wave speed, frequency, wavelength, hertz, metres per second, rearrange. Common mistakes: v = f + λ; mixing kHz with cm; using λ = v × f; thinking Hz × m is not m/s.","In the lab the learner reveals the equation, multiplies for v, divides for λ, converts units, spots the sum myth, and orders the method."]},"familyTip":"Ask: if a wave has frequency 5 Hz and wavelength 2 m, what is its speed? Then flip it: speed 340 m/s, frequency 680 Hz — what is λ?","keyIdea":"Wave speed v equals frequency f times wavelength λ (v = f × λ). Rearrange to λ = v ÷ f or f = v ÷ λ. Convert to Hz and metres before calculating so the product is in m/s.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Wave-equation idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["v = f × λ",0],["λ = v ÷ f",0],["v = f + λ",1],["convert kHz and cm first",0],["λ = v × f",1],["Hz × m gives m/s",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagvfl","caption":"Flagship lab: reveal v = f × λ, calculate speed, rearrange for λ, convert kHz/cm, catch sum/unit mix-ups, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zbF_WgVhgj":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A wave has a period of 0.25 s. What is its frequency in Hz?","answer":4,"hint":"How many quarter-seconds fit in one second?","explain":"f = 1 ÷ 0.25 = 4 Hz: four waves each second."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Calculate the period of a 440 Hz sound, in seconds. Give 2 significant figures.","answer":0.0023,"tolerance":6e-05,"hint":"Use T = 1 ÷ f, then round to 2 significant figures.","explain":"T = 1 ÷ 440 = 0.00227… s, which is 0.0023 s to 2 significant figures, about 2.3 ms."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A wave has a period of 20 ms. What is its frequency?","choices":["50 Hz","0.05 Hz","20 Hz"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change 20 ms into seconds before using f = 1 ÷ T.","explain":"20 ms = 0.02 s, and 1 ÷ 0.02 = 50 Hz. Using 20 instead of 0.02 gives the wrong 0.05 Hz."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A wave has a frequency of {f} Hz. How many waves pass a point in {t} seconds?","answer":"f*t","hint":"{f} Hz means {f} waves pass each second. Multiply by the number of seconds.","explain":"{f} waves each second × {t} s = {=f*t} waves.","params":{"f":[2,20],"t":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"UK mains electricity has a frequency of 50 Hz. What is its period?","choices":["0.02 s","50 s","0.5 s"],"answer":0,"hint":"T = 1 ÷ 50. Check your answer by multiplying it by 50.","explain":"T = 1 ÷ 50 = 0.02 s, and 50 × 0.02 = 1. 0.5 s is a decimal slip; 50 s multiplies instead of divides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"On a displacement-time graph, the time from one crest to the next crest is…","choices":["the period","the frequency","the wavelength"],"answer":0,"hint":"The horizontal axis is time. One crest to the next is one whole wave.","explain":"The time for one whole wave is the period. Frequency is 1 ÷ that time; wavelength is a distance, read from a distance graph."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A buoy bobs up and down {n} times in {=2*n} seconds. What is the frequency in Hz?","answer":"n/(2*n)","hint":"Frequency is waves per second: number of waves ÷ time.","explain":"{n} ÷ {=2*n} = 0.5, so f = 0.5 Hz. Each bob takes 2 s, and 1 ÷ 2 = 0.5.","params":{"n":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Wave A is 100 Hz and wave B is 200 Hz. Which has the longer period?","choices":["A, 0.01 s","B, 0.005 s","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fewer waves each second means each wave lasts longer.","explain":"T = 1 ÷ 100 = 0.01 s for A and 1 ÷ 200 = 0.005 s for B. The lower frequency has the longer period."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does a frequency of 1 Hz mean?","choices":["One wave passes each second","Each wave is 1 m long","Each wave is 1 m high"],"answer":0,"hint":"The hertz is a unit of 'per second'.","explain":"1 Hz is one wave, or one cycle, each second. Length is wavelength and height is amplitude."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{=k*s} waves pass a point in {s} seconds. What is the frequency in Hz?","answer":"k","hint":"Frequency is waves per second. Divide the number of waves by the time.","explain":"{=k*s} waves ÷ {s} s = {k} waves each second, so f = {k} Hz.","params":{"k":[2,9],"s":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A wave has a frequency of {=((a*a+a+2)/2)*(8*b*b-4*b+1)} Hz. What is its period in seconds?","answer":"1/(((a*a+a+2)/2)*(8*b*b-4*b+1))","hint":"T = 1 ÷ f. Check that f × T comes to 1.","explain":"T = 1 ÷ {=((a*a+a+2)/2)*(8*b*b-4*b+1)} = {=1/(((a*a+a+2)/2)*(8*b*b-4*b+1))} s. Check: {=((a*a+a+2)/2)*(8*b*b-4*b+1)} × {=1/(((a*a+a+2)/2)*(8*b*b-4*b+1))} = 1.","params":{"a":[0,2],"b":[0,2]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A wave has a period of 0.02 s. What is its frequency in Hz?","answer":50,"hint":"Use f = 1 ÷ T with T in seconds.","explain":"f = 1 ÷ 0.02 = 50 Hz. Check: 50 × 0.02 = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A wave's frequency doubles. What happens to its period?","choices":["It halves","It doubles","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"f × T = 1. If f gets twice as big, what must T do?","explain":"T = 1 ÷ f, so twice the frequency means half the period: 5 Hz to 10 Hz takes T from 0.2 s to 0.1 s."}],"explain":{"kid":["Frequency f is how many waves pass a point each second. Its unit is the hertz, Hz. 1 Hz means one wave each second, and 50 Hz means fifty.","The time period T is the time for one whole wave, crest to crest, in seconds. If 4 waves pass each second, each one takes a quarter of a second: T = 0.25 s.","So f and T are reciprocals: f = 1 ÷ T and T = 1 ÷ f. Multiply them and you always get 1. When the frequency doubles, the period halves.","Put T in seconds first. A period of 20 ms is 0.02 s, so f = 1 ÷ 0.02 = 50 Hz. A 440 Hz note has T = 1 ÷ 440, about 0.0023 s or 2.3 ms."],"adult":["Ages 13 to 15. GCSE physics (AQA 4.6.1.2) defines frequency as the number of waves passing a point each second, in hertz, and requires period = 1 / frequency, T = 1/f. The KS3 programme introduces frequency in hertz for sound and other waves. Key words: frequency, hertz (Hz), time period, oscillation, cycle, reciprocal, millisecond.","Common mistakes: treating frequency and period as the same number (T = 0.25 s so f = 0.25 Hz); thinking frequency and period rise together; reading 1 Hz as a length or a height; using milliseconds in f = 1/T (1 ÷ 20 = 0.05 Hz instead of 1 ÷ 0.02 = 50 Hz); decimal slips such as 1 ÷ 50 = 0.5; and multiplying instead of dividing (T = 440 s).","A quick check: f × T must equal 1 when T is in seconds. Estimating first helps too: many waves each second means each wave lasts well under a second. Frequency sets the pitch of a sound and the colour of light, and the period is what an oscilloscope trace shows from crest to crest.","In the lab students set a wave to 4 waves per second and see each wave last 0.25 s, fill a table for 1, 2, 4, 5 and 10 Hz, drag the end of one wave on a 0.1 s axis to T = 0.02 s and read 50 Hz, tune a 10 ms trace to 440 Hz, then match periods (including 20 ms) to frequencies with decoys for the usual slips."]},"familyTip":"Time 10 swings of a pendulum or a child on a swing with a phone stopwatch. Divide by 10 to get the period of one swing, then work out 1 ÷ T for the frequency. Try a shorter string or a smaller push and compare.","keyIdea":"Frequency f is the number of waves passing a point each second, measured in hertz (Hz): 1 Hz is one wave per second. The time period T is the time for one whole wave, in seconds. They are reciprocals: f = 1 ÷ T and T = 1 ÷ f, so f × T = 1. A period of 0.02 s gives 1 ÷ 0.02 = 50 Hz. A 440 Hz sound has T = 1 ÷ 440 ≈ 0.0023 s. Double the frequency and the period halves. Always put T in seconds before using f = 1/T: 20 ms is 0.02 s.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does each card describe frequency or period? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Frequency (Hz)","Period (s)"],"items":[["Waves passing each second",0],["Measured in hertz",0],["Goes up when the waves come faster",0],["Time for one whole wave",1],["Gap between crests on a time graph",1],["0.02 s for a mains wave",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaghz","caption":"Flagship lab: set waves per second, fill an f and T table, drag one wave to 0.02 s, tune 440 Hz and match periods to frequencies.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VBl1T1sFCM":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does vibrate mean?","choices":["Move back and forth very fast","Get louder and louder","Move one way and stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a plucked rubber band.","explain":"To vibrate is to move back and forth very fast, again and again."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"You hit a drum with a stick. What vibrates to make the sound?","choices":["The drum skin","The drumstick only","The drum legs"],"answer":0,"hint":"The stick hits something. What shakes after the hit?","explain":"The stick hits the skin, then the skin vibrates. That vibration makes the sound."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which part of a guitar vibrates to make the sound?","choices":["The strings","The round hole","The tuning pegs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part do you pluck with your fingers?","explain":"You pluck the strings and they vibrate. The hole only lets the sound out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Rice sits on a drum. You shout close to it and the rice jumps. Why?","choices":["The sound makes the drum skin vibrate","The rice heard the shout","Your breath blew it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sound can make things vibrate.","explain":"The sound travels through the air and makes the skin vibrate. The skin throws the rice up. Your breath is too far away to move it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A tuning fork hums but looks still. You dip it in water. What happens?","choices":["The water splashes","Nothing happens","The water gets hot"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is humming, so is it vibrating?","explain":"The fork vibrates too fast to see. In water, the fast shaking throws water up in a splash."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You pluck {a} bands. Then you touch {b} of them to stop them. How many bands still make a sound?","answer":"a-b","hint":"A band you touch stops vibrating, so it stops making sound.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b}. Only the bands still vibrating make a sound.","params":{"a":[5,9],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A drum stops vibrating. What happens to the sound?","choices":["It stops","It keeps going for ever","It gets higher"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sound is only made while something is vibrating.","explain":"The sound stops. Sound is only made while something is vibrating."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You stand far away and shout at the drum. The rice only wobbles. Why?","choices":["The sound is weaker when it gets there","Sound cannot reach a drum","The rice is too heavy to ever move"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened when you moved closer?","explain":"Sound spreads out as it travels, so it is weaker far away. Move closer and the skin vibrates more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A drum skin vibrates {n} times each second. How many times does it vibrate in {s} seconds?","answer":"n*s","hint":"Add {n} for each second, or work out {n} × {s}.","explain":"{n} times each second for {s} seconds is {n} × {s} = {=n*s} vibrations. That is too fast to count by eye.","params":{"n":[20,60],"s":[2,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You pluck a rubber band. What makes the sound?","choices":["The band moving back and forth fast","The air in the room getting warm","Your finger touching it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Watch the band after you let go. What is it doing?","explain":"The band vibrates: it moves back and forth very fast. That vibration makes the sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A band shakes back and forth {n} times each second for {s} seconds. How many shakes is that?","answer":"n*s","hint":"It does {n} shakes in each second. Add {n} for every second.","explain":"{n} shakes each second for {s} seconds is {n} × {s} = {=n*s} shakes. Vibrations are fast!","params":{"n":[10,50],"s":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A bell is ringing. You hold it tight with your hand. What happens?","choices":["The sound stops","The sound keeps going","The sound gets louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Your hand stops the bell moving.","explain":"Your hand stops the bell vibrating. No vibration means no sound."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {r} grains of rice on a drum. You shout close by and {j} of them jump. How many stay still?","answer":"r-j","hint":"Take the grains that jumped away from all the grains.","explain":"{r} take away {j} is {=r-j}. The sound made the skin vibrate and threw the other grains up.","params":{"r":[10,20],"j":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"When you hum, which part of you vibrates to make the sound?","choices":["Your voice box in your throat","Your ears","Your hair"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put your hand on your throat and hum. Where do you feel a buzz?","explain":"The voice box in your throat vibrates. You feel it as a buzz under your hand."}],"explain":{"kid":["Pluck a rubber band. It moves back and forth very fast. This is called a vibration. The vibration makes the sound.","Hold the band still. The sound stops. No vibration means no sound.","Lots of things vibrate to make sound. A drum skin shakes when you hit it. A guitar string shakes when you pluck it. When you hum, your voice box shakes. Put a hand on your throat and feel it buzz.","Sound can make things vibrate too. Put rice on a drum and shout close by. The sound makes the drum skin shake, and the rice jumps. Some things shake too fast to see. A humming fork in water makes a splash."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 9. In England, Year 4 science asks children to find out how sounds are made, and to link them to something vibrating. Children also learn that the vibrations travel through the air to the ear. In the US, NGSS 1-PS4-1 says vibrating things can make sound and sound can make things vibrate. Key words: sound, vibrate, vibration, source, travel, voice box.","Common mistakes: thinking the thing that hits makes the sound (the drumstick, not the drum skin); thinking the hole in a guitar makes the sound; thinking a sound goes on after the source stops vibrating; thinking a vibration must be big enough to see; and saying your breath made the rice jump. A careful test: hold a card in front of your mouth, or bang a pan lid instead. The rice still jumps.","This lesson is about where sound comes from and what it can do. Loud and quiet (how hard you hit) and high and low (how fast it vibrates) are other lessons. So the lab only asks: is it vibrating or not?","In the lab your child plucks a band and sees it blur, then touches it to stop the sound. They tap the part that vibrates on a drum, a guitar and a singer. They move a singer near or far from a drum with rice on it, then shout. Last, they tap a tuning fork on a rubber block and dip it in water."]},"familyTip":"Stretch a rubber band over an open box or tub and pluck it. Watch it blur, then touch it to stop the sound. Next, put a hand on your throat and hum, then stop: feel the buzz come and go. Last, stretch cling film over a bowl, sprinkle on some rice and bang a pan lid close by.","keyIdea":"Every sound is made by something vibrating: moving back and forth very fast. A plucked rubber band, a drum skin, a guitar string and the voice box in your throat all vibrate when they make a sound. When the vibrating stops, the sound stops. It works the other way too: sound travels through the air and can make other things vibrate, like a drum skin that throws rice up when you shout close to it. Some vibrations are too fast and small to see, but you can feel them or show them with water.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What vibrates to make the sound? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["This part vibrates","This part does not make the sound"],"items":[["The skin of a drum",0],["A guitar string",0],["The voice box in your throat",0],["The hole in a guitar",1],["Your ears",1],["The legs of a drum",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagvib","caption":"Flagship lab: pluck a band, stop it with a finger, find what vibrates, shout at a drum with rice on it, and dip a humming tuning fork in water.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_gLy3ZgZWiN":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why can you see yourself in a mirror but not in a carpet?","choices":["The mirror is smooth and shiny; the carpet is rough","The carpet is too far away","Mirrors make their own light"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how smooth each surface is.","explain":"A smooth, shiny mirror reflects light in one neat beam. Rough carpet sends light off in all directions."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is a light source?","choices":["A lit candle","A mirror","A shiny spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"A light source makes its own light, even in the dark.","explain":"A lit candle makes its own light. A mirror and a spoon only reflect light."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You crumple a flat sheet of foil. What happens to its reflection?","choices":["It breaks up: the bumps send light all over","It gets clearer","It stays just the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is crumpled foil smooth or bumpy?","explain":"Crumpled foil is still shiny, but its bumps send light in lots of directions. Smooth matters too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The wind blows across a pond. Why does the reflection of a tree go wobbly?","choices":["Ripples make the water bumpy","The tree is moving away","The water gets darker"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does the wind do to the top of the water?","explain":"Ripples make the water bumpy, so the light bounces off in different ways and the picture breaks up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Light from a torch hits a mirror. What happens to the light?","choices":["It bounces off","It stops dead","It goes through the mirror"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where the spot of light ends up.","explain":"The light bounces off the mirror. You can aim the bounced light at a wall."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A torch spot moves {a} steps up a wall, then {b} steps down. How many steps up from the start is it now?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Start with {a} steps up and take away the {b} steps down.","explain":"{a} take away {b} is {=a-b} steps up from where it began. Tilting the mirror moves the reflected spot.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which surface reflects the least light?","choices":["Black cloth","A mirror","A white tile"],"answer":0,"hint":"Dark, dull things send back very little light.","explain":"Black cloth is dark and dull, so hardly any light bounces back. That is why it looks dark."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"The Moon looks bright at night. Where does its light come from?","choices":["Sunlight reflected off the Moon","The Moon makes its own light","Street lights"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the Moon a light source, or does it reflect light?","explain":"The Moon makes no light. It reflects light from the Sun back towards us."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Sam's room has {k} mirrors with {s} shiny spoons, and {r} rough rugs. How many things in it give a clear reflection?","answer":"k+s","hint":"Count only the smooth, shiny things. Leave out the rugs.","explain":"{k} mirrors add {s} spoons is {=k+s}. The {r} rough rugs do not give a clear reflection.","params":{"k":[1,3],"s":[2,5],"r":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is it called when light bounces off a surface?","choices":["Reflection","A shadow","A light source"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the word for what a mirror does.","explain":"Light bouncing off a surface is called reflection. A mirror reflects light well."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Jas tests {a} smooth, shiny things and {b} rough things with a torch. How many things does she test?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the shiny things and the rough things together.","explain":"{a} add {b} is {=a+b} things. Only the {a} smooth, shiny ones give a clear reflection.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which of these gives the clearest reflection?","choices":["A still pond","A woolly jumper","A brick wall"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the smoothest, shiniest surface.","explain":"Still water is smooth and shiny, so it reflects light in one neat beam and you see a clear picture."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A class finds {n} things that reflect a clear picture. {m} of them are mirrors. How many are not mirrors?","answer":"n-m","hint":"Take the mirrors away from all the things they found.","explain":"{n} take away {m} is {=n-m}. Things like spoons, polished floors and still water reflect clearly too.","params":{"n":[8,12],"m":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You switch off every light in a room with no windows. What do you see in the mirror?","choices":["Nothing: there is no light to reflect","Your face, because mirrors shine","A bright glow"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does a mirror make light, or only bounce it?","explain":"A mirror makes no light. With no light to reflect, it shows nothing."}],"explain":{"kid":["Light comes from a light source, like the Sun, a lamp or a torch. When light hits something, it bounces off. We call this reflection.","Smooth, shiny things reflect light best. A mirror, a still pond and a polished floor send the light back in one neat beam. That is why you can see yourself in them.","Rough things, like carpet, bark or crumpled foil, send light off in lots of ways. You can not see a clear picture in them. Dark, dull things like black cloth hardly reflect any light.","A mirror does not make light. It only bounces light that falls on it. In a dark room, a mirror shows nothing. When wind makes ripples on a puddle, the water is bumpy, so its reflection breaks up."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. In England, Year 3 science asks children to notice that light is reflected from surfaces, and that we need light to see things. The NGSS also expects young children to test how light passes through or bounces off different materials (1-PS4-3). Key words: light, light source, reflect, reflection, surface, smooth, shiny, rough, dull, mirror.","Common mistakes: thinking shiny things such as a mirror, a spoon or the Moon make their own light; thinking light only reflects off mirrors (in fact most things reflect some light, which is how we see them, but rough ones scatter it); thinking light stops when it hits something; and thinking a crumpled piece of foil is as good as a flat one because both are shiny. Smooth matters as much as shiny.","The rule that light leaves a mirror at the same angle as it arrived comes later, in secondary school. Here the lab simply lets your child aim a torch so the bounced beam lands on a target.","In the lab your child aims a torch at a mirror to light up a star, tests six surfaces and compares how clear each bounce is, switches the torch off to see a mirror go dark, makes a puddle windy and calm, and finds the reflecting surfaces at home."]},"familyTip":"In a dim room, shine a torch at a mirror and move the spot of light around the walls. Then try a spoon, a sheet of foil (flat, then crumpled), white paper and a dark jumper. Which gives a bright, neat spot? Next time it rains, look for a reflection in a puddle and blow on it to make ripples.","keyIdea":"Light travels from a light source and bounces off the surfaces it hits. This bouncing is called reflection. Smooth, shiny surfaces such as a mirror, still water or a polished floor reflect light best: the light bounces off in one neat beam, so you can see a clear picture in them. Rough or dull surfaces like carpet, bark or paper send the light off in lots of directions, so there is no clear picture, and dark surfaces reflect very little. A mirror makes no light of its own: in the dark it shows nothing.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Could you see a clear reflection in it? Drag each card to a box, or tap the card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Clear reflection","No clear reflection"],"items":[["A flat mirror",0],["A still pond",0],["A polished floor",0],["A fluffy carpet",1],["Tree bark",1],["A crumpled paper bag",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshine","caption":"Flagship lab: bounce a torch beam onto a star, test six surfaces, switch the torch off, ripple a puddle and find reflections at home.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ydtcIBwHB9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why can you hear someone talking in the next room with the door shut?","choices":["The vibrations travel through the wall and door","The wall makes its own sound","Sound only travels through gaps"],"answer":0,"hint":"Walls and doors are solids. Can solids carry vibrations?","explain":"The voice makes the wall and door vibrate, and they pass the vibration on to the air on your side."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Through which does sound travel fastest?","choices":["Steel","Water","Air"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is a solid? Solids pass vibrations on fastest.","explain":"Sound is fastest in solids like steel (about 5,900 m/s), then water (about 1,480 m/s), then air (about 343 m/s)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Two astronauts on a spacewalk have broken radios. How could one hear the other?","choices":["Touch helmets and talk","Shout loudly","Talk slowly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Space has no air. What could carry the vibration instead?","explain":"Space is a vacuum, so shouting does nothing. Touching helmets lets the vibration travel through solid helmets."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A bell rings inside a jar. The air is pumped out. What do you notice?","choices":["The ring gets very quiet, but the bell still moves","The ring gets louder","The bell stops moving"],"answer":0,"hint":"The bell keeps working. What has gone from around it?","explain":"The bell still vibrates, but with almost no air there is almost nothing to carry the sound to you."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Sound travels about 1,480 m each second in water. About how far does it travel in {a} seconds?","answer":"1480*a","hint":"Multiply the distance in one second by the number of seconds.","explain":"1,480 × {a} = {=1480*a} m in {a} seconds.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Why does a string telephone work best when the string is tight?","choices":["A tight string passes the vibrations along","A tight string is louder because it is longer","A slack string lets air out"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a slack string do when you try to shake it?","explain":"A tight string carries the vibration from cup to cup. A slack string flops, so the vibration dies away."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mo says, 'In space sound goes further, because nothing gets in its way.' What is wrong?","choices":["Sound needs particles to travel, and space has almost none","Nothing, he is right","Sound in space is just quieter"],"answer":0,"hint":"What passes a sound from one place to the next?","explain":"Sound is passed on by particles. With no particles there is nothing to carry it, so no sound travels."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You put your ear on a long table and a friend taps the far end. Why is the tap clear?","choices":["The solid table carries the vibration well","Your ear is closer to the floor","Tables make taps louder by themselves"],"answer":0,"hint":"What material is between the tap and your ear?","explain":"The wood is a solid. Its particles are close together and pass the vibration on well, straight to your ear."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"Through steel, sound travels about 6 km each second. About how many kilometres does it travel in {a} seconds?","answer":"6*a","hint":"Multiply the 6 km for one second by the number of seconds.","explain":"6 × {a} = {=6*a} km in {a} seconds. In air it would cover only about {=a}/3 of a kilometre each second.","params":{"a":[2,7]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What carries the sound of a whale's song to another whale?","choices":["The water","The air above the sea","Nothing, sound cannot travel underwater"],"answer":0,"hint":"Whales sing underwater. What material is all around them?","explain":"The water's particles pass the vibration on. Sound travels well through water, even faster than through air."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Sound travels about 343 m each second in air. About how many metres does it travel in {a} seconds?","answer":"343*a","hint":"Each second adds another 343 m. Multiply 343 by the number of seconds.","explain":"343 × {a} = {=343*a} m in {a} seconds.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You pinch the middle of a tight string telephone. What happens to the sound?","choices":["It stops at your fingers","It gets louder","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can the string vibrate where you hold it still?","explain":"Holding the string stops it vibrating there, so the vibration cannot pass along to the other cup."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sound takes about 3 seconds to travel 1 km through air. A storm is {a} km away. About how many seconds after the lightning do you hear the thunder?","answer":"3*a","hint":"Each kilometre of air takes about 3 seconds. Multiply 3 by the number of kilometres.","explain":"3 × {a} = {=3*a}, so the thunder arrives about {=3*a} seconds after the flash. Light arrives almost at once.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"When a drum sends sound through the air, what do the air particles near your ear do?","choices":["Jiggle back and forth and pass the push on","Arrive from the drum","Stay perfectly still"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the red particle in the lab. Did it move all the way to the ear?","explain":"Each particle jiggles and bumps the next. The vibration travels; the particles stay roughly where they were."}],"explain":{"kid":["Sound starts when something shakes, or vibrates. The shake has to travel to your ear to be heard.","It travels through stuff: air, water, or a solid like wood or string. Each tiny bit of the stuff jiggles and bumps the next one. The bits do not fly to your ear. The bump does.","Sound goes faster in water than in air. It goes fastest in solids, like steel. You can hear a friend through a wall, and you can hear a boat when you swim underwater.","In space there is no air at all. Nothing is there to pass the shake on, so there is no sound. Astronauts use radios."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. This is the Year 4 sound objective in the National Curriculum in England: recognise that vibrations from sounds travel through a medium to the ear. Key words: sound, vibration, vibrate, medium, solid, liquid, gas, particle, vacuum, travel, ear, eardrum.","Sound is a vibration passed from particle to particle. In air at 20 °C it travels at about 343 m/s, in water about 1,480 m/s, and in steel about 5,900 m/s. Solids carry it fastest because their particles are packed closely and held tightly together. With no particles, as in a vacuum, there is nothing to pass the vibration on.","Common mistakes: thinking sound can only travel through air; thinking the air itself flows from the source to the ear; thinking sound travels further or more freely in space because nothing is in the way; and thinking a wall blocks all sound, when solids pass vibrations on well.","In the lab your child times the same drum through air, water and steel, follows one red particle that only jiggles, makes a string telephone work and then stops it with a pinch, pumps the air out of a jar around a ringing bell, and helps two astronauts talk by touching helmets."]},"familyTip":"Make a string telephone from two paper cups and a long piece of string. Talk with the string tight, then slack, then with someone pinching it. Another day, put your ear flat on a table while someone taps the far end gently, then lift your head and listen again.","keyIdea":"Sound is made by vibrations, and those vibrations travel through a material, called a medium, to your ear. The medium can be a gas like air, a liquid like water or a solid like wood, string or steel. Each particle jiggles and pushes the next one, so the vibration travels but the particles stay where they are. Sound travels faster through water than air, and fastest through solids. Where there are no particles at all, in a vacuum such as space, sound cannot travel.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"What carries the sound to the ear? Drag each to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A gas (air)","A liquid (water)","A solid"],"items":[["A friend calling across the playground",0],["A whale's song heard by another whale",1],["A voice along a string telephone",2],["A knock heard with your ear on a table",2],["A boat engine heard while swimming underwater",1],["Birdsong through an open window",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmedium","caption":"Flagship lab: time a drum through air, water and steel, follow one particle, try a string telephone, empty a bell jar and talk in space.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qZro923zvz":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why can you not see round a corner?","choices":["Light travels in straight lines","Light is too weak","Your eyes only face forwards"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the shape of the path light takes.","explain":"Light travels in straight lines, so light from round the corner cannot turn to reach your eyes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How do you see a book in a lit room?","choices":["Light bounces off it into your eyes","Light leaves your eyes and hits it","The book gives out its own light"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is a book a light source?","explain":"The book is not a light source. Light from a lamp or the Sun reflects off it and travels into your eyes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You move your hand closer to a torch. What happens to its shadow on the wall?","choices":["It gets bigger","It gets smaller","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Near the torch, does your hand block a wide spread of rays or a narrow one?","explain":"Close to the torch, the hand blocks a wide fan of rays, so the shadow gets bigger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ella says a shadow is a reflection of the object. Is she right?","choices":["No, it is where light is blocked","Yes, the object reflects its shape","Yes, but only in sunlight"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there more light or less light in a shadow?","explain":"A shadow is a dark area where an opaque object blocks the light. It is not a reflection."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"In a dark room with no light at all, can you see a red book?","choices":["No, no light reaches your eyes from it","Yes, if you look hard","Yes, red things glow"],"answer":0,"hint":"What has to enter your eyes for you to see something?","explain":"You see things only when light from them enters your eyes. With no light, the book cannot reflect any."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A toy {h} cm tall is a quarter of the way from a small lamp to a wall. How tall is its shadow, in cm?","answer":"4*h","hint":"By the wall, the rays have gone 4 times as far as at the toy.","explain":"The wall is 4 times as far from the lamp as the toy, so the shadow is 4 × {h} = {=4*h} cm.","params":{"h":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which object makes its own light?","choices":["A torch","A mirror","The Moon"],"answer":0,"hint":"A light source gives out light; other things only reflect it.","explain":"A torch is a light source. A mirror and the Moon only reflect light that comes from somewhere else."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A card is {h} cm tall. Its shadow is {=2*h} cm tall. How many times as far from the torch is the wall as the card?","answer":"2","hint":"Compare the shadow height with the card height.","explain":"{=2*h} ÷ {h} = 2, so the wall is twice as far from the torch as the card. Shadow size grows with distance in straight lines.","params":{"h":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A laser beam in a smoky room looks like a straight line. What does this show?","choices":["Light travels in straight lines","Smoke makes light","Light only works in smoke"],"answer":0,"hint":"The smoke lets you see the path of the light.","explain":"Smoke scatters a little light to your eyes from all along the beam, showing its path is straight."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Three cards with holes are lined up between a torch and your eye. The middle card moves sideways. What happens?","choices":["The light is blocked","The light bends to find the hole","The light gets brighter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can light change direction to go through a hole that is off the line?","explain":"Light travels in straight lines, so it cannot reach a hole that is off the line. The card blocks it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"In a ray diagram for seeing a book, which way should the arrows point?","choices":["Lamp to book, then book to eye","Eye to book","Book to lamp"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does the light start? Where does it end?","explain":"Light starts at the source, bounces off the book and ends in the eye. Arrows show that direction."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A card {h} cm tall is halfway between a small torch and a wall. How tall is its shadow on the wall, in cm?","answer":"2*h","hint":"The rays spread out in straight lines. By the wall they have gone twice as far as at the card.","explain":"The wall is twice as far from the torch as the card is, so the shadow is twice as tall: 2 × {h} = {=2*h} cm.","params":{"h":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Why does a cat-shaped card make a cat-shaped shadow?","choices":["Rays that just miss its edges go straight on","Light bends round the card","The card reflects a picture of itself"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to the rays that do not hit the card?","explain":"Rays that hit the card are blocked; rays that just miss its edges travel straight on. That traces the card's outline."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A card {h} cm tall is a third of the way from a small torch to a wall. How tall is its shadow, in cm?","answer":"3*h","hint":"By the wall, the rays have travelled three times as far as at the card, so they have spread three times as much.","explain":"The wall is 3 times as far from the torch as the card, so the shadow is 3 × {h} = {=3*h} cm tall.","params":{"h":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Light travels in straight lines. It cannot bend round corners. That is why you cannot see round a corner.","We see a lamp because its light travels straight into our eyes. We see a book because light from the lamp bounces off the book and then travels into our eyes. Our eyes do not send out light.","When something blocks the light, it makes a shadow. The rays that just miss the edge go straight on. So the shadow has the same outline as the thing that made it.","Move a toy closer to the torch and it blocks more of the spreading rays. Its shadow gets bigger. Move it closer to the wall and the shadow gets smaller."],"adult":["Ages 10 to 11. This is the Year 6 Light unit in the National Curriculum in England. Pupils recognise that light appears to travel in straight lines. They use this idea to explain that objects are seen because they give out or reflect light into the eye. They also use it to explain why shadows have the same shape as the objects that cast them. Key words: light source, ray, ray diagram, reflect, opaque, shadow, straight line.","Ray diagrams model light as straight lines with arrows pointing away from the source. To see a lit object, draw a ray from the source to the object and a second ray from the object to the eye. A small source gives a sharp shadow. Its size follows similar triangles: shadow height equals object height times the source-to-screen distance divided by the source-to-object distance.","Common mistakes: drawing the arrows from the eye to the object, as if eyes send out light; thinking we see objects without any light reaching the eye; thinking light bends round objects or fills in shadows; and thinking a shadow is a kind of reflection. Many children also expect a shadow to shrink as the object nears the light, when it grows.","In the lab your child lines up three holed cards so the beam reaches an eye, builds the ray paths for seeing a lamp and a book, swaps shaped cards to see matching shadows, slides a card to make its shadow twice as tall and then nearly card-sized, and predicts where a shadow's top edge will fall before switching on."]},"familyTip":"In a dark room, shine a torch through holes in three pieces of card lined up in a row, then nudge the middle one sideways and watch the light vanish. Then make hand shadows on a wall and move your hand towards and away from the torch. Ask each time: where do the straight rays go?","keyIdea":"Light travels in straight lines from a light source. We see a light source when its light enters our eyes, and we see other objects when light reflects off them into our eyes; eyes do not send light out. Because light cannot bend round an object, an opaque object blocks the rays that hit it and leaves a shadow with the same outline as the object. Moving the object closer to the light makes its shadow bigger.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"True or false? Drag each statement to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["True","False"],"items":[["Light travels in straight lines",0],["Our eyes send out light so we can see",1],["We see a book because light bounces off it into our eyes",0],["A shadow has the same outline as the object",0],["Light bends round objects to fill in shadows",1],["Moving a toy closer to the torch makes its shadow bigger",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagray","caption":"Flagship lab: line up holed cards, build the ray path for seeing a book, make shaped shadows, resize a shadow, and predict where its edge falls.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ZL9qVVnpwN":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What makes the string of a string phone carry your voice?","choices":["Your voice makes it shake","Water runs along it","Light shines down it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Put your hand on your throat and hum. What do you feel?","explain":"Your voice makes the cup shake, and the tight string carries the shake to the other cup."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A friend squeezes the string in the middle. What happens?","choices":["The message stops there","The message gets louder","Nothing changes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can the shake get past a tight squeeze?","explain":"The squeeze stops the string shaking, so the message stops where the hand is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You flash a torch 3 times, but Ben has no code. What does Ben know?","choices":["Only that you flashed","That you said come here","That you said yes"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who told Ben what 3 flashes means?","explain":"Without the code, Ben just sees flashes. You both need to agree what they mean."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The room is very noisy. Which signal is best?","choices":["A light Ben can see","A quiet whisper","A small bell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Noise covers up quiet sounds.","explain":"In a noisy room a sound gets lost, but Ben can still see a light."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your test fails. What does a good builder do next?","choices":["Find what went wrong, fix it, test again","Throw the phone away","Say it worked anyway"],"answer":0,"hint":"A failed test shows you where to look.","explain":"Find the part that stopped the message, fix it, and test again until it works."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Your code says 3 flashes means come here. You send it {n} times. How many flashes is that?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Each come here is 3 flashes.","explain":"{n} lots of 3 flashes is {=3*n} flashes.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why can a wall stop a torch signal?","choices":["Light goes in straight lines","Light is too quiet","Walls make light louder"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can light bend round the wall?","explain":"Light goes in straight lines, so the wall is in the way and blocks it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Ben is {d} steps away. He walks {k} steps back towards you. How many steps away is he now?","answer":"d-k","hint":"Take away the steps he walked towards you.","explain":"{d} − {k} = {=d-k}. He is {=d-k} steps away, so you sound louder to him now.","params":{"d":[6,9],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Ben is asleep with his eyes shut. How can you wake him?","choices":["Ring a bell","Flash a torch outside","Wave a flag"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can shut eyes see a flag?","explain":"Ring a bell. He cannot see with his eyes shut, but he can hear the sound."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ben walks a long way off. What happens to your voice when it gets to him?","choices":["It is quieter","It is louder","It is the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a friend calling you from far across a park.","explain":"A voice gets quieter the further it travels, so far away it is too quiet to hear."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Ben is {d} big steps away. He walks {k} more steps away. How many steps away is he now?","answer":"d+k","hint":"Add the new steps to the steps he was already away.","explain":"{d} + {k} = {=d+k}. Ben is {=d+k} steps away, so your voice will be even quieter.","params":{"d":[2,5],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Your string phone does not work. The string is floppy. What should you do?","choices":["Pull the string tight","Hold the string in your hand","Use a shorter cup"],"answer":0,"hint":"The shake has to run all the way along the string.","explain":"Pull it tight. A tight string carries the shake of your voice to the other cup."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Your torch code says 2 flashes means no. You send no {n} times. How many flashes is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Each no is 2 flashes, so count in twos.","explain":"{n} lots of 2 flashes is {=2*n} flashes.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Ben is round the corner of the house. Which signal can reach him?","choices":["A loud bell","A torch light","A wave of your hand"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one goes in straight lines and gets blocked by the wall?","explain":"A bell. Sound can spread round the corner, but light goes in straight lines and the wall blocks it."}],"explain":{"kid":["You want to tell Ben something, but he is far away. You shout. Your voice gets quieter as it goes. When he is too far, he hears nothing.","So you make a string phone. Talk into a cup. Your voice makes the cup shake. The shake runs down the string to Ben's cup, and he hears you. The string must be tight. If it is floppy, or a hand holds it, the shake stops.","At night you can use a torch. Flash it once for yes and two times for no. Ben must know the code too, or the flashes mean nothing to him.","Light goes in straight lines, so a wall stops it. Sound can go round a corner. In a loud place, a light is better. If your phone does not work, test it, fix it, and test it again."],"adult":["Ages 6 to 7. This is NGSS 1-PS4-4: use tools and materials to design and build a device that uses light or sound to solve the problem of communicating over a distance, with the K-2 engineering design practices of defining a problem, testing and comparing solutions (K-2-ETS1-1 and K-2-ETS1-3). It prepares for light in Year 3 and sound in Year 4 of the National Curriculum in England. Key words: signal, message, distance, vibrate, string telephone, torch, code, test, improve.","A string telephone works because the voice makes the cup bottom vibrate, and a taut string passes those vibrations to the other cup, which makes the air vibrate again. Anything that damps the string, such as slack, a pinch or the string resting on a fence, stops it. Light signals need a clear straight path to the eyes and an agreed code; sound spreads out, can bend round corners and through doors, but grows fainter with distance and is masked by noise.","Common mistakes: thinking sound stops at one point rather than fading; thinking a loose string works just as well; thinking flashes mean something by themselves, with no shared code; thinking light bends round a corner like sound; and giving up when a test fails. Be careful to call a failed test useful. It shows what to fix.","In the lab your child walks Ben out of hearing range. They pull a string phone tight and talk. They test a phone with three faults and fix each one where the message stops. They share a torch code, then send three messages. Last, they pick light or sound for six places."]},"familyTip":"Make a string telephone with two paper cups and a long piece of string. Try it with the string tight, then floppy, then with a finger pinching it. At bedtime, agree a torch code (1 flash for yes, 2 for no) and send each other messages across a dark room.","keyIdea":"Light and sound can carry a message a long way. A voice gets quieter the further it goes, so people build devices to help. A string telephone carries the shake of your voice along a tight string to a friend's cup. A torch can send flashes, but both people must agree the code first. Light goes in straight lines and is blocked by walls; sound can spread round corners but gets lost in noise, so you pick the signal that suits the place. Engineers test a device, find what went wrong, fix it and test again.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which signal works best here: a light you can see, or a sound you can hear? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Light signal","Sound signal"],"items":[["A friend is round a corner",1],["A dark night, far away",0],["A very noisy room",0],["A friend with eyes shut",1],["Behind a closed door",1],["A friend looking at your window",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsignal","caption":"Flagship lab: walk a friend out of earshot, pull a string phone tight, test and fix a broken one, flash a torch code, and pick light or sound for six places.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
